WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.780
A loop cannot decide that it has succeeded unless success has been defined in observable terms.

2
00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:13.137
If the goal is only "make this better," the loop has no stable reference point.

3
00:00:13.137 --> 00:00:18.646
It may rewrite working output, chase cosmetic improvements, spend budget on irrelevant actions,

4
00:00:18.646 --> 00:00:23.307
or declare completion based on a fluent summary rather than evidence.

5
00:00:23.307 --> 00:00:27.968
The task contract is the shared agreement that guides the loop.

6
00:00:27.968 --> 00:00:33.054
It should identify the intended outcome, permitted scope, available inputs, expected outputs,

7
00:00:33.054 --> 00:00:37.291
operational constraints, acceptance criteria, required evidence, failure conditions, escalation conditions,

8
00:00:37.291 --> 00:00:38.562
and resource limits.

9
00:00:38.562 --> 00:00:43.224
The contract does not need to be a long legal document.

10
00:00:43.224 --> 00:00:51.275
It needs to be precise enough that planning, tool use, evaluation, and termination all point to the same objective.

11
00:00:51.275 --> 00:00:58.056
It also gives reviewers a compact way to decide whether the loop stayed inside its assignment.

12
00:00:58.056 --> 00:01:06.531
In the coding example, a vague goal is "improve this code." A stronger contract is "make the three identified tests

13
00:01:06.531 --> 00:01:13.311
pass without changing the public interface or adding dependencies." That statement provides a target, a boundary,

14
00:01:13.311 --> 00:01:15.006
and an evaluation path.

15
00:01:15.006 --> 00:01:21.786
The loop may inspect selected files, edit within the repository, and run the approved test command.

16
00:01:21.786 --> 00:01:26.024
It may not deploy, retrieve secrets, or modify unrelated directories.

17
00:01:26.024 --> 00:01:27.719
Completion also needs evidence.

18
00:01:27.719 --> 00:01:33.228
A model statement that the task is complete is a claim, not proof.

19
00:01:33.228 --> 00:01:39.584
Useful evidence might be a passing test, valid schema, successful build, matching checksum, policy approval,

20
00:01:39.584 --> 00:01:42.127
accepted diff, or explicit human acceptance.

21
00:01:42.127 --> 00:01:48.483
When evidence is unavailable, the loop should report uncertainty rather than convert confidence into completion.

22
00:01:48.483 --> 00:01:52.721
This distinction prevents fluent status messages from replacing engineering verification.

23
00:01:52.721 --> 00:01:56.535
A strong loop has more than a success state.

24
00:01:56.535 --> 00:02:02.891
It may stop because the task succeeded, required information is missing, current access is insufficient,

25
00:02:02.891 --> 00:02:10.095
a policy blocked the next action, the budget was exhausted, progress stopped, or human review is required.

26
00:02:10.095 --> 00:02:15.181
"Continue until done" is unsafe because "done" has not been made measurable.

27
00:02:15.181 --> 00:02:22.808
Clear stop conditions keep the system from confusing motion with progress and give every later component a contract

28
00:02:22.808 --> 00:02:24.080
it can enforce.
